    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function spiralOrder(matrix) {\n  if (matrix.length === 0) return [];\n  const result = [];\n\n  let top = 0;\n  let bottom = matrix.length - 1;\n  let left = 0;\n  let right = matrix[0].length - 1;\n\n  while (top <= bottom && left <= right) {\n    for (let c = left; c <= right; c++) result.push(matrix[top][c]);\n    top++;\n\n    for (let r = top; r <= bottom; r++) result.push(matrix[r][right]);\n    right--;\n\n    if (top <= bottom) {\n      for (let c = right; c >= left; c--) result.push(matrix[bottom][c]);\n      bottom--;\n    }\n\n    if (left <= right) {\n      for (let r = bottom; r >= top; r--) result.push(matrix[r][left]);\n      left++;\n    }\n  }\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The two extra guards"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Why do the third and fourth loops need `if` checks when the first two do not?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Because the boundaries move **during** the iteration. Consider a single-row matrix: the first loop consumes it and increments `top` past `bottom`. Without the guard, the bottom-row loop would run again over the same row, duplicating every value."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The first two loops are safe because the `while` condition was just checked. After `top++` and `right--`, it no longer holds — so it must be rechecked."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Complexity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time: O(R x C)** — every cell is pushed exactly once."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Space: O(1)** beyond the output."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The pattern of shrinking boundaries generalises to any ring-by-ring processing, including rotating layers in place."
    }
  ],
